Trail is the project record that lives inside a project folder, like.git, and as one main task keeps track of the datasets a project uses and what happens to them across tools.
 In initiative I described three places Trail listens: the kernel (IPython cell events), JupyterGIS (map signals), and the OS (file events).
 For Thursday I only want the OS part and the resource tracking.

What I want to see working end to end on a small toy project: initialize Trail in a folder, register two or three files as resources,
change one of them outside the notebook (overwrite it, rename it, delete it), and have Trail report what changed and to which resource. That is the whole prototype. If that works cleanly we build the rest on top of it.

A few hints so you don't spend time on things I could easily tell you . Think git, not a daemon.
Git does not watch your files, it diffs on demand when you run git status. Start there: record size and mtime when a
resource is registered, and compare against the current file when asked. OS file events (watchdog or similar) can come
later as an optional layer for immediacy, they are not the foundation. Keep the store dumb: an append only log where each line is one record,
with a handful of resource records and event records. No database, no fancy schema, no CRDT. Register resources explicitly, one call or one command per file.
Do not auto register everything in the folder, that would sweep in notebooks, scripts, .env files etc as if they were data. IDs: the project gets one ID set once at init,
and each resource gets its own local ID inside the project. Don't derive ID from the path or the file name, those change and the ID has to survive that. A random ID with a
uniqueness check at creation is enough for now. Project ID can be very simple for now, we make it robust later.

Dont overengineer this. Standard library only if you can, a few small modules, and a couple of tests that prove the scenario above.
I care more about the design being right than the code being complete. Bring what you have till Thursday and we go through it together. (edited) 
